h2(doc, "1.2  Summary of the transaction as documented")
para(doc, "The Loan is a $47,500,000 construction-to-mini-permanent facility secured by a first deed of trust on two contiguous parcels at 601 and 615 Foster Street, Durham, North Carolina (Durham County PINs 0821-04-72-3341 and 0821-04-72-4102; 3.47 acres combined), improved with a six-story, 212-unit mixed-use project containing 18,400 square feet of retail and a 276-space structured garage. The Borrower is Harborview Capital Partners LLC. The Guarantors are Elena Vasquez-Torres (62% member and Managing Member) and Reginald K. Osei (10% member). A $9,200,000 mezzanine loan from Brightleaf Mezzanine Capital LLC, which is under common control with the 28% member Brightleaf Investors Group LLC through Thomas R. Chen, sits behind the senior Loan and is secured by a pledge of 100% of the Borrower\u2019s membership interests. Total Project Costs are $56,700,000. Target closing is February 28, 2025.")

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 2
h1(doc, "2.  EXECUTIVE SUMMARY")
para(doc, "The transaction is underwritable, but not on the record as it currently stands. Six issues are gating: they either prevent closing as documented, defeat a covenant on its face, or would expose the Bank to a loss that the credit approval does not price. The remaining issues are resolvable through documentation, additional deliverables, or Senior Loan Committee confirmation of a business point.")
para(doc, "The gating issues are:")
sub(doc, "(i)", "The Loan cannot comply with its own credit tests. The Term Sheet caps loan-to-cost at 80% of Total Project Costs and as-completed loan-to-value at 65%, but sizes the Loan at $47,500,000 against costs of $56,700,000 and an as-completed value of $72,800,000. Those figures produce 83.77% LTC and 65.25% LTV. The Credit Memorandum records both numbers and marks the LTC a \u201cFail,\u201d but marks the LTV a \u201cPass\u201d by measuring it against a 70% maximum that appears nowhere in the Term Sheet. Its proposed cure \u2014 a $2,140,000 equity escrow \u2014 does not change either ratio, because an escrow deposit is neither a reduction of the Loan nor an increase in Total Project Costs (Issues A-1, A-2, A-7).")
sub(doc, "(ii)", "The Borrower has no equity in the transaction, and the land is not equity. The Term Sheet describes the previously acquired land as the Borrower\u2019s contribution; the Credit Memorandum describes it as the only equity in the capital stack and computes an 18.2% equity position that includes it. The Borrower\u2019s own detailed budget funds the entire $8,200,000 land cost from Mezzanine Loan proceeds and funds $1,000,000 of the Developer Fee from Mezzanine Loan proceeds as well. On the Borrower\u2019s numbers, the senior and mezzanine loans together equal 100% of Total Project Costs, and the sponsor recovers the land investment and a fee at closing with no cash at risk (Issues A-3, A-4, A-6).")
sub(doc, "(iii)", "The Borrower\u2019s minimum net worth covenant cannot be satisfied after closing. With $56,700,000 of Total Project Costs funded by $56,700,000 of debt, the Borrower\u2019s book equity after full draw is approximately zero, against a covenant of $12,000,000. The stated $14,200,000 net worth is not reconcilable to the $8,200,000 of documented member contributions, and the Credit Memorandum\u2019s claimed $2,200,000 cushion therefore does not exist (Issue B-1).")
sub(doc, "(iv)", "The environmental land use restriction conflicts with the building program and the required deed notice appears not to have been recorded. NCDEQ conditioned its No Further Action determination on restricting 0.38 acres to non-residential use, and the plans place 14 residential units above that area. Separately, the NFA Letter required a deed notice to be recorded by November 29, 2024; the title commitment dated December 5, 2024 does not reflect it, while the Appraisal asserts that it is recorded. Either the Borrower is in breach of an NFA condition, or the title commitment is incomplete (Issues D-1, D-2).")
sub(doc, "(v)", "Title cannot be delivered as required. The Loan requires a first-priority deed of trust subject only to four Permitted Exceptions, but an unreleased 2018 deed of trust securing $3,400,000 remains of record, its outstanding balance is not established anywhere in the file, and there is no budget line and no cash equity available to pay it. In addition, demolition (August 2024) and remediation (September 2024) were performed before the deed of trust will be recorded, so lien claims from that work may prime the Bank under N.C.G.S. \u00a7 44A-10. The Appraisal states that the historic district architectural review approval has been obtained; the title officer states there is no evidence of it (Issues E-1, E-3, E-5).")
sub(doc, "(vi)", "The pledge of membership interests is claimed twice at the first-priority level, and the operating agreement gives an affiliate of the mezzanine lender control over the Borrower\u2019s bankruptcy decision. Both the senior Loan and the Mezzanine Loan are stated to be secured by a pledge of 100% of the membership interests. The required intercreditor agreement has been negotiated but not executed. More significantly, the operating agreement requires a 75% member vote for a voluntary bankruptcy filing; Ms. Vasquez-Torres (62%) and Mr. Osei (10%) hold only 72%, so Brightleaf Investors Group LLC \u2014 whose affiliate is the mezzanine lender \u2014 holds the deciding vote over any filing (Issues C-1, C-3).")

